{{also|hacke|hacké}}
==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈhakə/}}
* {{audio|de|De-Hacke.ogg|Audio (Berlin)}}
* {{hyph|de|Ha|cke}}

===Etymology 1===
15th century, from {{der|de|gml|hakke}}, from {{der|de|gem-pro|*hak(k)-|}}, perhaps related to {{m|gem-pro|*hakô}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|hak}}. The word also existed in {{cog|goh|-}} as {{m|goh|hakka||animal heel}}, but this had no attested continuation in [[w:Middle High German|Middle High German]].

====Noun====
{{de-noun|f}}

# {{lb|de|regional|chiefly|northern and central Germany}} [[heel]] (of the foot or shoe)
#: {{syn|de|Ferse}}

=====Usage notes=====
* There is now a tendency to use {{m|de||Hacke}} especially for the back of the shoe (i.e. that part which is around the heel of the foot), whereas the body part is more often called {{m|de|Ferse}} even in the north. Such use may include or exclude the [[sole]] of a high-heeled shoe ({{m|de|Absatz}}). In the south the word is rare in either sense.

